@* Table's Overview, written by hand after Button's: the URL-first behavior (sorting, paging,
   filters) previewed in each mode, selection in a form, then state in the component (Live only),
   data from a database, row links and the look as code. From docs/components.md (Table) and the
   dev host's TableDemo. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Sorting and paging</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Sortable</Code> on a column and <Code>PageSize</Code> on the table. Headers and page numbers are links that set <Code>?sort=</Code> and <Code>?page=</Code>, so the table works with no script, survives a reload and can be shared.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-sort-page" />
<TextBlock><Code>InitialSort</Code> sets the order before anyone picks one (<Code>"-date"</Code> for newest first), and <Code>PageSizeOptions</Code> adds a "Rows per page" menu.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Column filters</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Filterable</Code> on a column for a filter in its header. Text matches anywhere, numbers exactly, a date its day, and enums and booleans get a menu.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-filters" />
<TextBlock>Filters go in the URL as <Code>?f-customer=ada</Code>. <Code>FilterMultiple</Code> lets an enum column match several values, and <Code>FilterPlacement.Row</Code> puts the filters in a row under the headers. For filters of your own, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/filter-form">FilterForm</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Selection</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Selectable</Code> with an <Code>ItemKey</Code> for a checkbox per row. In a form, the checked rows post their keys, so this works with JavaScript off.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-selection" />
<TextBlock>Read the keys with <Code>[SupplyParameterFromForm(Name = "selected")]</Code> on a static page, or <Code>@@bind-SelectedKeys</Code> on an interactive one. Give columns <Code>FooterText</Code> for a totals row.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">State in the component</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>@@bind-Sort</Code>, <Code>@@bind-Page</Code> and <Code>@@bind-Filters</Code> on an interactive page to keep them in C# instead of the URL, like a client-side grid.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-bound" />
<TextBlock>The table starts from the URL if it has values, and a new sort or filter goes back to page 1. Set the page back to 1 yourself when your own filters change.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Data from a database</Heading>
<TextBlock>Pass an EF Core <Code>IQueryable</Code> as <Code>Items</Code>, and the database sorts, filters and pages. For more control, use <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> to load each page yourself:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Provider</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Row links</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>RowHref</Code> to make each row a link, and <Code>RowLabel</Code> to name it. Links, buttons and checkboxes inside the row still work on their own.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@RowLinks</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Several tables on a page</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>QueryPrefix</Code> to give each table its own keys in the URL, so they sort and page on their own.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Prefix</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Lines, size and scrolling</Heading>
<TextBlock>Use <Code>Size</Code>, <Code>Striped</Code>, <Code>ColumnLines</Code>, <Code>RowLines</Code> and <Code>Bordered</Code> for the look; turn <Code>Bordered</Code> off for a table in a card. <Code>MaxHeight</Code> scrolls the rows under a sticky header, and <Code>EmptyText</Code> shows when there are none.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Look</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<Heading Level="2">See also</Heading>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/filter-form">FilterForm</Anchor>: search and filters of your own, in the URL.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/pagination">Pagination</Anchor>: page links for a list that isn't a table.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: state in the URL on static pages.</ListItem>
</List>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        <Table Items="orders" PageSize="10" Caption="Orders">
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Customer" Sortable Filterable />
            <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Total" Format="C" Sortable />
            <TemplateColumn Title="Status">
                <Badge>@context.Status</Badge>
            </TemplateColumn>
        </Table>
        """;

    private const string Provider = """
        <Table TItem="Order" ItemsProvider="LoadOrders" PageSize="10">...</Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<TableResult<Order>> LoadOrders(TableRequest<Order> request)
            {
                var query = db.Orders.Where(o => o.Active);
                var page = await request.Apply(query).ToListAsync();   // filters, sorts, pages
                return new(page, await request.ApplyFiltering(query).CountAsync());
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string RowLinks = """
        <Table Items="orders" RowHref='o => $"orders/{o.Id}"' RowLabel='o => $"order {o.Number}"'>...</Table>
        """;

    private const string Prefix = """
        <Table Items="orders" QueryPrefix="o-" PageSize="10">...</Table>     @* ?o-sort=, ?o-page= *@
        <Table Items="refunds" QueryPrefix="r-" PageSize="10">...</Table>
        """;

    private const string Look = """
        <Table Items="orders" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Striped ColumnLines>...</Table>
        <Table Items="orders" MaxHeight="Length.Rem(20)" EmptyText="No orders yet">...</Table>
        """;
}
